@@ -226,3 +226,48 @@ externalized builtin. A formal benchmark was waived by project decision
 | Durability | store dies with the ENGINE process | store dies with the WORKER process (ADR 0001; file_based/redis unchanged) |
 | Latency | in-process µs | engine⇄worker WS round-trip (low ms) — formal benchmark waived by project decision 2026-07-06 |
 | Telemetry (`track_state_*`) | engine-internal counters | none — out of scope for this migration; lands with the shared worker observability story |
+
+## Personal compatibility extension: compact write acknowledgement
+
+This local candidate adds `state::set_ack` with the same input and mutation,
+validation, reserved-scope protection, and event behavior as `state::set`.
+It returns only JSON `true` after success and propagates the same errors.
+`state::set` retains its existing old/new-value response. This avoids echoing
+large source records across the RPC connection when the caller only needs an
+acknowledgement. An ACK is **not** a disk-flush or crash-durability receipt;
+AgentMemory's separate persistence verification must remain enabled.
+
+This is a custom extension of the `state/v0.22.22` source tag, not an official
+State Worker release. It can be removed only when the official worker provides
+an equivalent bounded response and passes the same compatibility tests.
+
+### Local boot identity / instance aliases (migration evidence only)
+
+`state::boot_identity` returns immutable Linux boot facts: `version:1`, `pid`,
+`start` (process start ticks as a decimal string), `instanceId` (boot UUID),
+`bootId`, `adapter`, and canonical `storePath` (null for non-file stores).
+The path is taken from the constructed adapter, not the seed configuration.
+No credentials or complete configuration are returned. Unavailable process or
+path evidence returns `BOOT_IDENTITY_UNAVAILABLE`; it never fabricates evidence.
+
+The same handlers also register under `state::<instanceId>::` for `set`,
+`set_ack`, `compare-and-set`, `barrier`, `get`, `delete`, `update`, `list`,
+`list_keys`, `list_groups`, and `boot_identity`. These directly share the canonical
+handlers and guards, rather than forwarding through a replaceable canonical RPC.
+Canonical APIs remain unchanged; private namespace claims remain canonical.
+Clients can independently validate the boot identity, then pin these aliases so a
+replacement worker does not silently receive their calls.
+
+Identity is NOT authentication, a single-writer lock, or a durability receipt.
+Consumers must validate executable/process/socket/store evidence separately.
+This lifecycle defense is against accidental replacement and stale routing, not
+an adversary with the same OS user/root authority or arbitrary engine registration.
+
+File-backed local workers also hold an exclusive, nonblocking advisory lock on
+`.agentmemory-state.lock` in the canonical store directory before loading state.
+Contention fails startup before registering RPCs. The lock file is never removed
+or truncated; it is separate from AgentMemory's application writer lease. The
+open lock stays owned by the shared flush mutex, including background and pending
+blocking flush tasks, until all writer references end or the process exits.
+This excludes cooperating patched workers only. Older/unpatched processes do not
+honor this advisory lock and must not be run against the same store.
